🔹 🎯 Task 1: Base Case vs Recursive Case
You are practicing the structure of a basic recursive method.
📥 Input:
int n;
⚙️ Requirements:
• Create a method factorial(int n) using recursion
• Base case: n == 0 returns 1
• Recursive case: n * factorial(n - 1)
• Print: "Factorial: <value>"
📌 Constraints:
• Use only standard Java
• Output must match the required format exactly
📤 Output format:
Factorial: <value>
📌 Test cases:
Input: n=5
Output:
Factorial: 120
Input: n=0
Output:
Factorial: 1</value></value>

🔹 🎯 Task 2: Recursion on Arrays
You are finding the maximum value using recursion.
📥 Input:
int[] arr;
⚙️ Requirements:
• Create a method max(int[] arr, int index)
• Base case: index == arr.length - 1 returns arr[index]
• Recursive case: return max of arr[index] and max(arr, index + 1)
• Print: "Max: <value>"
📌 Constraints:
• Use only standard Java
• Output must match the required format exactly
📤 Output format:
Max: <value>
📌 Test cases:
Input: arr = [3, 7, 2, 9, 5]
Output:
Max: 9
Input: arr = [-4, -8, -1]
Output:
Max: -1</value></value>

🔹 🎯 Task 3: String Recursion
You are checking if a string is a palindrome recursively.
📥 Input:
String s;
⚙️ Requirements:
• Create a method isPalindrome(String s, int left, int right)
• Base case: left >= right returns true
• If s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right) return false
• Recursive case: call isPalindrome(s, left + 1, right - 1)
• Print: "Palindrome: <value>"
📌 Constraints:
• Use only standard Java
• Output must match the required format exactly
📤 Output format:
Palindrome: <value>
📌 Test cases:
Input: s="level"
Output:
Palindrome: true
Input: s="hello"
Output:
Palindrome: false</value></value>